          "content": "## Synthesis case: competing geological explanations\n\nA team is examining a reservoir abutment affected by fractured carbonate zones. The investigation was conducted while long-term seepage was relatively quiet, creating a risk that the observed state is not representative of the critical construction condition. The team has a preliminary map, two discrete observations and a short monitoring record. Neither spatial continuity nor transient behavior is fully constrained.\n\nConstruct three competing cross-sections: one with laterally continuous units, one with an abrupt structural boundary, and one with a buried erosional or weathering feature. For each cross-section, list the predicted drill response, the predicted groundwater response, and the construction problem that would arise if it were true. Rank only the **value of information**, not a numerical probability unsupported by data. Identify the most efficient next test and the observations that would cause you to revise the construction method.\n\nFinally write a limitations paragraph for a geotechnical or engineering-geology report. This paragraph must say what was measured, what was inferred, the dates and groundwater conditions, and which assumptions need checking during construction. Separate a local observation from a regional geological generalization and make all units consistent."
